// LeafProvenance is where one leaf of an attachment's configuration came
// from: whether the author wrote it explicitly or an annotation supplied
// it, which annotation, and — when an explicit value took precedence
// over an annotation-supplied one — which annotation it displaced.
//
// It is one fact rather than several because the question a check asks is
// a single one with several coordinates: an explicit value silently
// displacing an annotation is only visible from the displaced side and the
// displacing side together.
type LeafProvenance struct {
	// Origin is how the leaf's value arrived: authored explicitly, or supplied
	// by an annotation.
	Origin string

	// AnnotationKey is the annotation that supplied the value, empty when the
	// author wrote it explicitly.
	AnnotationKey string

	// AnnotationSource names where that annotation was declared.
	AnnotationSource string

	// DisplacesAnnotation reports whether an explicit value took precedence
	// over an annotation-supplied one.
	DisplacesAnnotation bool

	// DisplacedAnnotationKey is the annotation the explicit value displaced,
	// empty when none was displaced.
	DisplacedAnnotationKey string

	// DisplacedAnnotationSource names where the displaced annotation was
	// declared.
	DisplacedAnnotationSource string
}

// View is the base every stage's view embeds. It declares no fact of its
// own; it carries the contract every view shares.
//
// A view exposes named facts and never a raw document. There is no
// accessor returning an authoring document, a graph, or a pointer to an
// authored entity type. That single property is what makes re-derivation
// impossible: a check handed no raw structure has nothing to derive from,
// so its declaration describes what it reads exhaustively rather than
// approximately.
//
// A check may compose declared facts; it may not reach behind them.
// Composition is reading one accessor and filtering its result through
// another — a check wanting the populated sockets iterates the
// declared-name accessor and filters through the population predicate.
// That is the only thing a check can do, and it is legitimate and
// unavoidable; nothing here forbids it. What is closed is re-derivation:
// reaching behind an accessor to the underlying document to compute a fact
// the accessor does not name.
//
// Accessors take arguments rather than being bound to a subject, because
// facts are keyed by tuples — per node and socket, per node and slot,
// per attachment and pillar and leaf — and an argument-taking accessor
// states its key in its signature.
//
// One fact, one accessor, with no convenience variants. A fact exposed
// both as a predicate and as a set-returning form would be two code paths
// for one question. An accessor may return a composite record where the
// fact itself is composite; that is one fact, not several.
//
// Host powers — the filesystem, the plugin cache, workspace
// configuration — are never facts and never appear here. They are
// declared on a separate axis, so a view cannot be the route to one. See
// [NoHostPowers].
type View interface{}

